  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule is enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`kibana_sample_data_flights`, ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) has 33 fields. The main ones are `timestamp` (date), `Cancelled` (boolean), `Carrier` (string), `FlightDelay` (boolean), `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `Origin`/`Dest` fields, and `DestLocation`/`OriginLocation` (geo_point). The time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-5cbba7ee Flights' (ID `c25261c6-cb2d-45e6-94bd-d55b99226cd0`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view. One is an XY line chart of count over `timestamp`. The other is an XY bar chart of count split by `Carrier`. My first create request was rejected by validation (a `size` field on the terms axis), so nothing was created, and the retry succeeded.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-5cbba7ee Cancelled flights' (ID `c64417b7-94ec-4e16-abf7-728e6a528c46`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1m, counts documents where `Cancelled : true` in a 5m window, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it.\n4. **Panel rename:** the carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I checked that the dashboard still has two panels. The rule reads back as enabled.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- The carrier panel was created as \"Flights per carrier\" and then renamed, as step 4 asks.\n- The rule has no actions attached, so it will evaluate and record alerts but send no notifications.\n- The rule uses the Discover-style search source on the flights data view, with a `kuery` filter, rather than a raw DSL query.\n- The rule's consumer is `stackAlerts`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
